WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.216
Insert, twist, weld.

2
00:00:03.216 --> 00:00:06.276
Lecture four of the Manufacturing module.

3
00:00:07.288 --> 00:00:09.590
Lecture three made our 96 hairpins.

4
00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:11.976
Now they become one winding:

5
00:00:11.976 --> 00:00:17.940
into the slots, legs twisted, and the ends welded in pairs.

6
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:23.044
Our example is still the EV-size stator, 48 slots and 8 poles.

7
00:00:23.704 --> 00:00:24.504
The steps:

8
00:00:24.288 --> 00:00:30.058
line the slots, set the pins in a nest, insert them, widen and twist

9
00:00:30.058 --> 00:00:34.084
the legs, weld the ends, then check, connect and coat.

10
00:00:34.872 --> 00:00:40.500
The angles, heights and weld positions come from our Hairpin Motor Designer, with the

11
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.180
formed crowns of lecture three.

12
00:00:43.253 --> 00:00:44.327
Chapter one.

13
00:00:44.327 --> 00:00:45.793
Liners and the nest.

14
00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:53.243
First every slot gets its liner, folded insulation between copper and steel:

15
00:00:53.243 --> 00:00:59.700
often a laminate of aramid paper and polyimide film, sold as Nomex and Kapton,

16
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.853
0.13 to 0.2 millimetres thick.

17
00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:08.428
A machine creases the paper, folds it, pushes it into the slot and cuts

18
00:01:08.428 --> 00:01:12.479
it, leaving a few millimetres standing out at each end.

19
00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:15.096
One maker quotes 1.3 seconds a slot:

20
00:01:15.096 --> 00:01:17.245
about a minute for our 48 slots.

21
00:01:18.225 --> 00:01:22.854
Meanwhile the pins are set into a nest, a ring of grooves wider than

22
00:01:22.854 --> 00:01:23.654
the slots.

23
00:01:23.404 --> 00:01:25.408
The nest can be loose;

24
00:01:25.408 --> 00:01:26.574
the slots cannot.

25
00:01:26.574 --> 00:01:31.798
So each pin finds its place in the nest, and the full set, the

26
00:01:31.798 --> 00:01:33.757
basket, goes into the core together.

27
00:01:34.833 --> 00:01:38.738
The 48 pins of layers one and two form a ring.

28
00:01:38.738 --> 00:01:43.372
Most span five slots and overlap like roof tiles, so the last ones must

29
00:01:43.372 --> 00:01:44.575
go under the first.

30
00:01:44.575 --> 00:01:50.401
One patent parks the first few on a spacer, sets the rest, slips the

31
00:01:50.401 --> 00:01:53.884
last few under, then pushes the first ones home:

32
00:01:53.884 --> 00:01:56.797
on our ring, five, 38 and five.

33
00:01:57.777 --> 00:02:02.209
Then the second ring, layers three and four, the same way.

34
00:02:02.209 --> 00:02:05.829
In one study, automated setting took one to two seconds a pin;

35
00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:07.773
by hand, about ten.

36
00:02:08.868 --> 00:02:09.979
Chapter two.

37
00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:11.184
Insert and widen.

38
00:02:12.672 --> 00:02:17.552
A gripper lifts the basket and lines up all 192 legs over the 48

39
00:02:17.552 --> 00:02:20.396
lined slots, four legs to a slot.

40
00:02:21.184 --> 00:02:26.526
In our stator a 3.6-millimetre bar enters its lined slot with 0.15 millimetres to

41
00:02:26.526 --> 00:02:27.861
spare on each side.

42
00:02:27.861 --> 00:02:33.727
The chamfered leg ends find the slot, and guides hold the liner against the

43
00:02:33.727 --> 00:02:37.644
walls, so no leg can catch it and drag it in.

44
00:02:38.656 --> 00:02:43.809
The legs go in part way, then a press seats the whole basket, and

45
00:02:43.809 --> 00:02:47.653
a stop sets how far the legs stand out.

46
00:02:47.653 --> 00:02:50.116
Each leg travels about 190 millimetres:

47
00:02:50.116 --> 00:02:53.932
through the 120-millimetre core, and some 70 out the other side.

48
00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.120
Rejects at this step:

49
00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:02.332
a bent crown, scratched enamel, a leg standing too high or too low, or

50
00:03:02.332 --> 00:03:04.556
a liner torn or pushed out of place.

51
00:03:05.536 --> 00:03:09.913
On the weld side the legs now stand 61 to 76 millimetres out of

52
00:03:09.913 --> 00:03:14.144
the core, the even layers taller, as they have further to twist.

53
00:03:14.144 --> 00:03:19.521
Then ring tools widen them, moving each layer out a little to make room

54
00:03:19.521 --> 00:03:20.684
for the twist tool.

55
00:03:25.792 --> 00:03:27.023
Chapter three.

56
00:03:27.023 --> 00:03:27.823
Twist.

57
00:03:29.084 --> 00:03:32.846
The twist tool is a set of rings, one per layer.

58
00:03:32.846 --> 00:03:37.795
Each ring has a pocket at every slot, and each pocket takes one leg

59
00:03:37.795 --> 00:03:38.595
tip.

60
00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:39.528
Our stator needs four rings.

61
00:03:40.188 --> 00:03:44.719
Lecture two showed that with a five-slot crown, the weld side must span seven

62
00:03:44.719 --> 00:03:45.519
slots.

63
00:03:45.019 --> 00:03:46.824
That is the weld pitch.

64
00:03:46.824 --> 00:03:52.387
Seven does not halve, so the odd layers turn three slots one way, and

65
00:03:52.387 --> 00:03:54.331
the even layers four the other.

66
00:03:54.331 --> 00:03:56.520
Three plus four is seven:

67
00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.696
each leg meets its partner.

68
00:03:59.324 --> 00:04:04.580
On our 48-slot stator that is 22.5 degrees for the odd layers and 30

69
00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:05.960
for the even ones.

70
00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.533
Opposite turns have a second benefit.

71
00:04:09.533 --> 00:04:13.251
One thesis puts each ring at hundreds to thousands of newton-metres.

72
00:04:13.251 --> 00:04:16.457
Turned opposite ways, their torques mostly cancel;

73
00:04:16.457 --> 00:04:22.222
one layer alone would push its whole torque through the core and its clamp,

74
00:04:22.222 --> 00:04:23.912
which can damage the laminations.

75
00:04:24.828 --> 00:04:30.603
As the tip swings round, the leg leans over, so the tip must also

76
00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:31.403
come down.

77
00:04:31.246 --> 00:04:36.329
In our stator the drop is 12 to 27 millimetres, less for the three-slot

78
00:04:36.329 --> 00:04:37.535
layers.

79
00:04:37.535 --> 00:04:40.637
This drop against angle is the twist curve;

80
00:04:40.637 --> 00:04:43.208
machines follow it with cams or servo axes.

81
00:04:44.092 --> 00:04:45.343
Each leg bends twice:

82
00:04:45.343 --> 00:04:50.676
at its base, where it leans over, and at the tip, which the pocket

83
00:04:50.676 --> 00:04:52.185
holds upright.

84
00:04:52.185 --> 00:04:56.838
We gave the base bend the gentle radius of the crown shoulders, an inner

85
00:04:56.838 --> 00:04:58.088
radius of 7.2 millimetres.

86
00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:03.326
Then springback again, calculated as in lecture three.

87
00:05:03.326 --> 00:05:09.842
The layer-one leg springs back about 0.9 degrees and its layer-two partner 1.1, so

88
00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:12.777
their tips move 0.7 and 1.1 millimetres.

89
00:05:12.777 --> 00:05:19.423
Partners twist opposite ways, so they spring apart sideways, along the row:

90
00:05:19.423 --> 00:05:20.680
about 1.8 millimetres.

91
00:05:21.244 --> 00:05:27.049
So the rings turn a little too far, then back off slightly before letting

92
00:05:27.049 --> 00:05:27.849
go.

93
00:05:27.376 --> 00:05:30.429
One research group's model overtwists by one degree:

94
00:05:30.429 --> 00:05:32.392
about 1.3 millimetres at our tips.

95
00:05:33.244 --> 00:05:38.218
After the twist our tips stand 49 millimetres above the core.

96
00:05:38.218 --> 00:05:43.072
The gentle bends cost 3.8 millimetres of height against sharp drawing corners.

97
00:05:48.011 --> 00:05:49.147
Chapter four.

98
00:05:49.147 --> 00:05:49.975
Laser welding.

99
00:05:51.463 --> 00:05:53.095
Our stator has 90 welds.

100
00:05:53.095 --> 00:05:58.715
42 pairs in the inner row, 42 in the outer, and six switched welds

101
00:05:58.715 --> 00:06:02.407
that link the two rows, as in lecture two.

102
00:06:02.407 --> 00:06:05.523
The same core with eight layers would need 186.

103
00:06:06.439 --> 00:06:08.484
First each pair is clamped together.

104
00:06:08.484 --> 00:06:15.382
Before clamping, the partners may stand a few tenths of a millimetre apart, face

105
00:06:15.382 --> 00:06:16.182
to face.

106
00:06:16.063 --> 00:06:21.173
In a 2023 study by Omlor and colleagues, the weld tolerated gaps only up

107
00:06:21.173 --> 00:06:22.195
to 0.6 millimetres.

108
00:06:22.983 --> 00:06:26.584
Some machine makers also cut the tips to one height.

109
00:06:26.584 --> 00:06:31.380
That levels the scatter from twisting, though flat cut ends reflect more laser light

110
00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.851
than the chamfered ones.

111
00:06:33.639 --> 00:06:35.361
Why a laser?

112
00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:40.208
Copper carries heat away fast, so the energy must come in a short, intense

113
00:06:40.208 --> 00:06:41.286
burst.

114
00:06:41.286 --> 00:06:46.218
In the Omlor study the laser put about four and a half kilowatts into

115
00:06:46.218 --> 00:06:47.827
a spot 0.17 millimetres wide.

116
00:06:48.871 --> 00:06:53.437
Solid copper reflects most of an infrared beam and absorbs about five percent.

117
00:06:53.437 --> 00:06:59.828
Once it melts, a keyhole opens, a narrow channel of vapour, and the copper

118
00:06:59.828 --> 00:07:01.128
absorbs about 70 percent.

119
00:07:01.128 --> 00:07:07.679
Green light is absorbed better, but in one benchmark it welded four times slower,

120
00:07:07.679 --> 00:07:08.979
though with fewer pores.

121
00:07:09.863 --> 00:07:16.082
A scanner, a pair of fast mirrors, steers the beam from pair to pair.

122
00:07:16.082 --> 00:07:21.320
A camera finds each pair, then the beam runs small loops across both tips,

123
00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:23.795
an ellipse about 2.4 by 1.2 millimetres.

124
00:07:24.615 --> 00:07:28.079
In the Omlor study a weld took about 110 milliseconds:

125
00:07:28.079 --> 00:07:33.831
for our 90 welds, ten seconds of laser time, a little more for our

126
00:07:33.831 --> 00:07:35.295
thicker bar.

127
00:07:35.295 --> 00:07:38.515
One machine maker allows 50 to 200 milliseconds a pair.

128
00:07:39.559 --> 00:07:41.097
What goes wrong?

129
00:07:41.097 --> 00:07:41.897
Spatter:

130
00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:46.611
drops of molten copper thrown out, which can land on the enamel.

131
00:07:46.611 --> 00:07:47.674
And pores:

132
00:07:47.674 --> 00:07:50.153
gas trapped as the weld freezes.

133
00:07:50.153 --> 00:07:55.161
The gas comes from enamel left on the copper, as lecture three warned, and

134
00:07:55.161 --> 00:07:57.043
from oxygen in ordinary tough-pitch copper.

135
00:07:58.023 --> 00:07:58.823
Two fixes.

136
00:07:58.694 --> 00:08:03.830
In the Omlor study, oxygen-free copper cut the pore volume by 40 percent.

137
00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:09.303
A ring of light around the main spot keeps the keyhole open.

138
00:08:09.303 --> 00:08:14.422
On oxygen-free copper it cut pores by another two thirds, and the weld tolerated

139
00:08:14.422 --> 00:08:15.763
gaps of 0.8 millimetres.

140
00:08:16.647 --> 00:08:19.182
How big must a weld be?

141
00:08:19.182 --> 00:08:23.280
The joint must have at least the area of the conductor.

142
00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:28.271
Our copper is about 14 square millimetres and the joint 3.4 millimetres wide, so

143
00:08:28.271 --> 00:08:30.771
the weld must reach about 4.2 millimetres deep.

144
00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:37.130
Chapter five.

145
00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:38.978
Check, connect, coat.

146
00:08:40.466 --> 00:08:44.547
One bad weld can scrap the stator, so every weld is checked.

147
00:08:44.547 --> 00:08:48.072
During welding, sensors watch the light from the melt.

148
00:08:48.072 --> 00:08:53.727
After it, a 3D sensor measures the bead, the solid drop of copper on

149
00:08:53.727 --> 00:08:54.527
each pair.

150
00:08:55.154 --> 00:08:57.063
Resistance is the electrical check.

151
00:08:57.063 --> 00:09:00.870
One study measured good joints at about three micro-ohms;

152
00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:04.126
a one-millimetre gap pushed them above eight.

153
00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:06.217
Then the leads.

154
00:09:06.217 --> 00:09:10.951
Our stator has two parallel paths per phase, so twelve ends have no partner:

155
00:09:10.951 --> 00:09:16.494
six join the three phase leads, and six the neutral ring, which joins the

156
00:09:16.494 --> 00:09:18.501
phases in a star.

157
00:09:18.501 --> 00:09:19.739
More laser welds:

158
00:09:19.739 --> 00:09:21.022
102 joints in all.

159
00:09:21.714 --> 00:09:23.974
Last, the beads are coated.

160
00:09:23.974 --> 00:09:29.162
They are bare, live copper, 6 to 7.5 millimetres from the next pair, which

161
00:09:29.162 --> 00:09:30.477
may be another phase.

162
00:09:30.477 --> 00:09:36.100
So the stator is heated and its beads dipped into epoxy powder, which melts

163
00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:38.043
onto the hot copper.

164
00:09:38.043 --> 00:09:41.246
Some lines coat only after impregnation, in lecture five.

165
00:09:42.465 --> 00:09:43.265
Summary.

166
00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:44.045
Four points.

167
00:09:45.117 --> 00:09:45.917
One.

168
00:09:45.403 --> 00:09:50.519
The pins are set in a loose nest, the last under the first, then

169
00:09:50.519 --> 00:09:52.233
pushed into the lined slots together.

170
00:09:53.053 --> 00:09:53.880
Two.

171
00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:59.039
Odd and even layers twist opposite ways, three and four slots, so each leg

172
00:09:59.039 --> 00:10:01.897
meets its partner and the torques mostly cancel.

173
00:10:02.685 --> 00:10:03.485
Three.

174
00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:09.322
Springback shifts partners sideways, so the rings overtwist, and each pair is clamped before

175
00:10:09.322 --> 00:10:10.122
welding.

176
00:10:10.525 --> 00:10:11.325
Four.

177
00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:15.313
A laser welds each pair in about a tenth of a second.

178
00:10:15.313 --> 00:10:20.038
Residue and oxygen make pores, and one bad weld can scrap a stator, so

179
00:10:20.038 --> 00:10:21.193
every weld is checked.

180
00:10:22.429 --> 00:10:25.262
That is the end of lecture four.

181
00:10:25.262 --> 00:10:26.580
Next, lecture five:

182
00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:30.614
impregnate and test, how the winding is sealed and proven.

183
00:10:30.614 --> 00:10:35.684
Until then, insert, twist and weld our stator step by step in the free

184
00:10:35.684 --> 00:10:36.484
Viewer.

185
00:10:36.797 --> 00:10:41.565
Motoreka is a product of UMPSA, developed at EMDLab.

186
00:10:41.565 --> 00:10:43.721
motoreka.my.

